{{also|moon-calf}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|moon-calf}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|moon|calf|lang=en}}, after a superstition that the moon caused abnormal fetal development.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈmuːnkɑːf/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|mooncalves}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} An abnormal [[mass]] within the uterus; a [[false]] [[conception]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* c. '''1611''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|The Tempest}}'', Act II, Scene 2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=tempest&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: Thou art very Trinculo indeed! How camest thou to be the [[siege]] of this '''moon-calf'''? Can he vent Trinculos?
# A poorly-conceived [[idea]] or [[plan]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# A [[dreamer]], someone [[absent-minded]] or distracted; a [[fool]], [[simpleton]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* '''1902''', {{w|John Kendrick Bangs}}, ''Olympian Nights'', New York: Harper & Bros., Chapter 10, p. 185,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/olympiannights00banguoft]</sup>
#*: “{{...}} you’re a jobbernowl and a doodle, a maundering '''mooncalf''' and a blockheaded numps, a gaby and a loon; you’re a ''Hatter!''” I shrieked the last epithet.
#* '''1957''', {{w|Ogden Nash}}, “Come On In, The Senility Is Fine” in ''You Can’t Get There From Here'', Boston: Little, Brown & Co., p. 66,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/youcantgettheref00nash]</sup>
#*: But I can think of no one but a '''mooncalf''' or a gaby
#*: Who would trust their own child to raise a baby.
#* '''1978''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''{{w|Livia (novel)|Livia}}'', Faber & Faber, 1992 (''Avignon Quintet''), p. 463,
#*: He slipped it softly onto her unresisting finger and, like the unwise '''moncalf''' he was, kissed it.

